        <section id="overview" class="intro">
          <h1>Discover the robot’s<br /><em>geometry through motion.</em></h1>
          <p class="lead">Odometry estimates position and heading from measurements collected over time. Begin with the editable Euler model supplied in StampC3_Template: acquire encoder readings, use the supplied printed measurement sheet to calibrate wheel radius and separation, and test each change against physical observations. Then investigate gyroscope heading and, as a Going Further exercise, displacement from acceleration.</p>
          <h3>Familiarising with the Odometry Model</h3>
                                <p>Your copy of <code>StampC3_Template</code> contains <code>OdometryModel.h</code>, which has the source code for a complete but simple kinematic model in the form of <strong>Euler integration</strong>.  Euler integration means that time is made discrete - we update the model in time steps.  The simple model provided also approximates the overall continuous motion of the robot in two discrete steps.  First, translation - moving the robot forwards or backwards along the current heading.  Second, applying a rotation to reorientate the robot, which will then serve as the heading for the next update iteration. </p>   
                                                          <figure class="plot"><img src="./assets/images/odometry/discrete_updates_sketch.png" width="50%" alt="" /><figcaption>Figure 1. An illustration of how a continuous curve trajectory is estimated by a discrete straight-line approximation.  The discrete nature of the approximation has been greatly exaggerated to illustrate the concept.</figcaption></figure>
          
                               <p> When the robot is powered on, it's initial position is configured as the origin (\(x = 0, y = 0, \theta=0\)).  At heading zero the robot faces along +x; positive rotation turns towards +y, which we would observe as anticlockwise rotation when viewed from above.</p>
                                
                                <figure class="plot"><img src="./assets/images/odometry/local_frame_sketched.png" width="50%" alt="" /><figcaption>Figure 1. Coordinate frame assumption, by default the robot initialises itself to be at the coordinate frame origin.</figcaption></figure>
                                <p>The model has 4 essential parameters: the time step, the wheel radius, the wheel separation, and the number of encoder counts per wheel revolution (CPR).  The CPR should be considered as correct and fixed - the manufacturer of the robot specifies this as 358.3 counts per revolution.  Your task across the following exercises is to first improve the odometry on board the robot, and then measure and evaluate the overall performance.  Your findings will then be carried over into Phase 2, the group project element on the Digital Twin simulation.</p>
                       
                       <figure class="plot"><img src="./assets/images/odometry/model_parameters_sketch.png" width="50%" alt="" /><figcaption>Figure 1. Coordinate frame assumption, by default the robot initialises itself to be at the coordinate frame origin.</figcaption></figure>    
                       
<p>Importantly, for our odometric model, we do not want to know the total count of encoder pulses since the robot was powered on.  The total count for each motor does not describe the potential complexity of the motion path taken by the robot. For example, some turning movements (rotating on the spot) or reverse manoeuvrers will cause the encoder count to decrease, not just increase.  Inspecting only the total count does not represent these motion dynamics.   Instead, our odometric model will update in discrete time steps - repeated measurements taken with a spacing of a small interval of time.  The odometric model therefore computes the difference between two encoder counts spaced by an interval of time.  We can understand this as asking <i>"how far did the wheel travel in this time step?"</i>.   This allows for a piecewise estimation of how the robot moved to be constructed iteratively.</p>
                
                <p>In these exercises you are guided to develop code for the StampC3 within the Controller_c class (Controller.h file).  The Controller_c class framework implements a 10ms interval in the update() function, which in turn called the runLineFollower() example function. This 10ms schedule was created to allow the Pololu 3Pi+ robot sufficient time to execute it's own code to read sensors, control motors, respond to I2C requests, and so on.  The Controller_c class is also written to be directly compatible with the Digital Twin simulation code in Processing - which you will work with closely in Phase 2 of this course.  Therefore, as much as possible you should preserve the general design of the Controller_c class. That way, you will be able to directly transfer the control code for your robot between the real robot and the simulated robot.</p>     
                    

          
         
