          <article id="exercise-01" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">01</div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div><span class="concept-tag">Acquisition · a supplied model</span><h2>Reading encoders and estimating motion</h2></div>
                <span class="time-badge">35–45 min</span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p><strong>Goal:</strong> understand the translation of encoder counts through the odometry class, produce a reusable record of odometry measurements for later use.</p>
                
<p>Each motor on the robot is fitted with a quadrature encoder.  The encoder has a disc magnet which rotates above a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hall_effect_sensor">Hall Effect sensor</a>.  This means that when the motor shaft rotates an electronic pulse is created that can be counted.  More information on how the encoders work can be found in the <a href="https://www.pololu.com/docs/0J83/5.4">official documentation</a>.  In this course, counting the pulses and determining whether the wheel is rotating forwards or backwards has been implemented for you.  The robot will register a change in wheel rotation and either increment or decrement the rotation count.
                </p>
                <p>To read the current encoder count for the motors, we will utilise a helper function within the <strong>Robot_c</strong> class (defined in Robot.h). In Robot_c, find 
                <blockquote>getEncoders()</blockquote>  Reviewing the code in this function, we can observe that the function requests a "snapshot".  You can find this snapshot (encoders) and other snapshot variables declared at the top of the Robot_c class.  This snapshot terminology reflects the fact that the encoder values retrieved were correct at the time they were requested, but that they may have since changed.  In real terms, if a snapshot of the encoder counts is retrieved, but the robot continues to move, then we expect that the count stored on the robot itself will have continued updating.</p>
                
                <p>Two more helper functions, <strong>getLeftEncoderCount()</strong>, and <strong>getRightEncoderCount()</strong>, are provided to attempt to reduce potential confusion about which count represents which wheel.  Reviewing the function getLeftEncoderCount(), we can observe that it has the <strong>return type</strong> of <strong>int32_t</strong>.  This means that the function will return a <strong>signed integer</strong> number value, whose range is represented across <strong>32 bits</strong>.  In actuality, 1 bit is reserved to represent the sign of the number (-/+) whilst the remaining 31 bits represent the numeric value.  Suffice to say, an int32_t can store very large number values.</p>
                
                <p>Counts of rotation produced by the encoder may seem confusing - how does this map to a rotation or distance travelled?  The official documentation for the <a href="https://www.pololu.com/docs/0J83/5.4">encoders</a> tells us that one whole rotation of the wheel produces 358.3 encoder counts.  We can therefore work out either how much rotation occurs for 1 encoder count ( degrees: \(\frac{360}{358.3}\), or radians: \(\frac{2\pi}{358.3}\)), or how much distance would be travelled by the wheel for one encoder count (wheel circumference divided by CPR, \(\frac{2 \pi r}{358.3}\)).  </p>      
                                
                                <p>However, our robot moves itself with two wheels, and their combined interactions determine the resultant motion of the whole robot.  Therefore, capturing the distance travelled by a single wheel is only the initial step in the process of modelling the motion. If one wheel rotates and the other does not, the robot will pivot (turn about) on the wheel that is not rotating.  If both wheels rotate forwards at the same speed, the robot should move forwards.  If both wheels rotate in opposite directions with the same speed, the robot should rotate on the spot.  Therefore, it is necessary to also model the essential physical geometry of the robot, and to combine this with the independent rotation counts for the left and right wheels.</p>
                              
                                <strong>Odometry</strong> is the name given to this process of estimating overall motion from input measurements such as these. Developing and improving the odometry is your main task in this complete set of exercises.  When we discuss the physical geometry of the robot and the mathematical operations of the model, we may use the phrase <strong>kinematics</strong>.  In this first exercise, your task is to simply familiarise with the code provided to you, to verify that the example code works, and to make your first assessment of the accuracy and precision of the odometry by reporting odometry outputs.</p>
                                
                               
                <p>Begin at the top-level <code>StampC3_Template.ino</code>, which creates <code>robot</code>, <code>imu</code>, and <code>controller</code>. In <code>Controller.h</code>, the public <code>controller.odometry</code> object receives counts after a successful <code>robot.getEncoders()</code> request. That request returns a success flag and refreshes cached data. <code>getLeftEncoderCount()</code> and <code>getRightEncoderCount()</code> then return the named wheel values. Calling a getter alone does not acquire another observation.</p>
                
                <p>The supplied <strong>first-order Euler</strong> update approximates movement during that interval as a straight step along the previous heading, followed by a heading change:</p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Euler pose update uses the previous heading for x and y, then adds heading change">
                \[\hat{x}_{k+1}=\hat{x}_k+\Delta s\cos\hat{\theta}_k,\quad
                \hat{y}_{k+1}=\hat{y}_k+\Delta s\sin\hat{\theta}_k,\quad
                \hat{\theta}_{k+1}=\hat{\theta}_k+\Delta\theta\]
                </div>
                <p>For example, equal wheel travel of 10 mm at heading zero adds 10 mm to x and leaves y and heading unchanged. During a curve, the real heading changes throughout the interval, so longer steps can make this approximation less accurate. The equations match the pose update in the Processing <code>DigitalTwin/OdometryModel.pde</code>, although this StampC3 class uses measured encoder changes. Count changes already describe movement over an interval: do not multiply the resulting distances by elapsed time again.</p>
                <p>Keep the two available pose estimates distinct. <code>controller.odometry.pose</code> is your editable local estimate; <code>robot.pose</code> is received from the 32U4 by <code>robot.getPose()</code>. The controller's existing Serial and WiFi pose columns report the local estimate. Neither estimate is an independent measurement of the robot's position.</p>
              </div>
              
