          <article id="exercise-02" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">02</div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div><span class="concept-tag">Translation · effective radius and residuals</span><h2>Wheel radius and distance calibration</h2></div>
                <span class="time-badge">55–70 min</span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p><strong>Goal:</strong> use the supplied printed measurement sheet to estimate an effective shared wheel radius, apply it to the supplied Euler model, and test whether it improves distance estimates on measurements excluded from calibration.</p>
                
					 <p>The investigation and development of the kinematic model should begin with the wheel radius.  You can review OdometryModel.h to become familiar with the mathematical steps of the kinematic update as they are described in code.  The two components of the update are <strong>translation</strong> (moving the robot position forwards or backwards linearly) and <strong>rotation</strong>.</p>
					 
					 <p>The translation component, \(\Delta s\), a linear change in position, is calculated with the equation:</p>
					 
                <div class="equation" align="center" role="img" aria-label="Forward distance is the mean wheel distance; heading change is right minus left wheel distance divided by separation">
                \(\Delta s=\frac{s_L+s_R}{2}\)
                </div>                
                <p>where \(S_{L}\) is the distance travelled by the left wheel, and \(S_{R}\) is the distance travelled by the right wheel.  In this simple model implementation, the total distance travelled in one time step is the mean of \(S_L\) and \(S_R\).  Recall that the robot can determine the distance a wheel travels by calculating the difference in encoder counts, \(\Delta n\), for a given time step:</p>
                       <div class="equation" align="center" role="img" aria-label="Forward distance is the mean wheel distance; heading change is right minus left wheel distance divided by separation">
                \(S_{L} = \frac{2 \pi r}{N} \Delta n\)
                </div>
                <p>where \(r\) is the nominal wheel radius (assumed), \(N\) is the total encoder counts per revolution (CPR, 358.3). We can interpret the above equation as calculating the distance (of the wheel circumference) travelled for 1 encoder count, and then multiplying that value by the difference in encoder counts.</p>
                <p>Rotation is calculated with the equation:</p>
                <div class="equation" align="center" role="img" aria-label="Forward distance is the mean wheel distance; heading change is right minus left wheel distance divided by separation">
                \(\Delta\theta=\frac{s_R-s_L}{B}\)
                </div>                
                <p>In the above equation, the term \(B\) in introduced, which is the physical distance between the two wheels.  \(B\) is the subject of investigation in the next exercise. The equation above is ratiometric. The numerator is a measurement of length - the difference in the distance travelled by each wheel.  The denominator is the length between the two wheels.  Therefore, The amount the robot turns depends on the difference in wheel travel relative to the distance between the wheels. For each timestep, \(\Delta\theta\) provides a quantity of rotation in radians. </p>
                <p>In this discussion, we are interested in investigating both \(r\) and \(B\), and we have a choice about which to investigate first.  In the case of translation, \(B\) is not involved, and therefore \(r\) can be studied in isolation.  Once a good estimation of \(r\) is found, rotation can be investigated with \(B\) in relative isolation (depending on our confidence of \(r\)). 
                </p>

					<h3>A Test Procedure</h3>
					<p>To calibrate the value \(r\) (wheel radius) we will need to make external measurements of the robot, and to carefully place the robot at the beginning of each test.  Therefore, this process involves human activities and can be described as <strong>experimental procedure</strong>.  When reporting an experiment, it is important to declare and discuss limitations in the overall approach.  Clearly, a human involved within an experiment introduces uncertainties and potentially subjectivities.</p>
					<p>To calibrate the wheel radius we will require the wheel to rotate a known distance, and then to check what distance was calculated by the odometry model in comparison.  Keeping the CPR fixed, a difference in distance travelled will correlate to a misrepresented radius in the odometry model.  However, there are other variables that influence this relationship, such as whether the wheel is rotated to start and stop precisely, whether it maintains consistent contact with the surface, and so on. For example, placing the robot may cause a tiny wheel rotation.</p>
					
					<p>To move rotate a wheel a precise distance we have a couple of potential approaches.  First, the robot could be flipped over, with the wheels facing up and not in contact with the surface.    
                <!--
                <p>The printed sheet gives you a consistent external reference for position and heading, so you do not need to design a separate measurement apparatus. It is still a measurement tool rather than perfect ground truth. Printer scaling, how the robot is placed, the chassis point chosen for alignment, and how a marking is read can all change the reported pose.</p>
                
                <p>Check at least one stated dimension on the printout with a ruler. Use the coordinate axes and alignment markings provided, and identify the robot point that represents the model's x and y position—ideally the midpoint between the wheel contacts. A marker on the front of the chassis moves around that midpoint during a turn, so treating it as the robot centre would create apparent translation. Record both the starting and final physical pose rather than assuming every placement begins exactly on the nominal mark.</p>
                <p><strong>Placement and reading uncertainty</strong> is the variation seen when the robot is repeatedly placed and read at the same intended pose. It differs from movement repeatability, which also includes variation in how the robot moved. A tightly grouped set of placements can support a useful resolution estimate, but it cannot reveal every systematic error: a printout scaled incorrectly or a consistent viewing angle can remain repeatable and biased. Keep these limitations visible when judging the calibration.</p>
                <p>A wheel with a larger circumference travels farther per revolution, provided it rolls without slipping. The number of encoder counts per revolution, \(N\), is fixed for this exercise: it describes how wheel rotation is reported, not a quantity to tune. The wheel radius is less certain. Manufacturing variation, tyre compression, and the interaction between the tyre and test surface can make the distance travelled per revolution differ from that predicted by a nominal radius. If the model's radius is too small, it will tend to underestimate travel, with the discrepancy increasing over longer distances.</p>
                <p>Let \(r_{\mathrm{nom}}\) be the nominal wheel radius and \(g_r\) a dimensionless radius correction. Their product defines the <strong>effective wheel radius</strong>, \(r_{\mathrm{eff}}\), used by the model. For a signed encoder-count change \(\Delta n\), the estimated wheel travel is:</p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Effective wheel radius equals a dimensionless radius correction times nominal radius; estimated wheel travel equals two pi times effective radius times signed count change divided by the fixed counts per revolution">
                \[r_{\mathrm{eff}}=g_r r_{\mathrm{nom}},\qquad
                \hat{s}=\frac{2\pi r_{\mathrm{eff}}}{N}\Delta n
                =\frac{2\pi g_r r_{\mathrm{nom}}}{N}\Delta n\]
                </div>
                <p>Here \(g_r=1\) retains the nominal radius, while a value above or below one increases or decreases it proportionally. This makes the correction physically interpretable: it is applied to the uncertain radius rather than to the encoder count. In <code>OdometryModel.h</code> there is no separate gain field, so the value ultimately entered into <code>leftWheelRadiusMm</code> and <code>rightWheelRadiusMm</code> is \(r_{\mathrm{eff}}\).</p>
                <p>For a nearly straight run with a shared radius, use the signed mean count change \(q=(\Delta n_L+\Delta n_R)/2\). Fitting measured distance against \(q\) gives a slope \(m\), in millimetres per count:</p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Estimated forward distance equals the fitted slope times mean count change; effective radius is the slope times fixed encoder counts per revolution divided by two pi; radius correction is effective radius divided by nominal radius">
                \[\hat{d}=mq,\qquad r_{\mathrm{eff}}=\frac{mN}{2\pi},\qquad
                g_r=\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}}{r_{\mathrm{nom}}}\]
                </div>
                <p>The slope is an intermediate result that connects the observations to the radius; it is not evidence that the encoder resolution has changed. For example, if a nominal radius of 16.0 mm required \(g_r=1.03\), the effective radius would be 16.48 mm. With \(N=358.3\), the model would then predict approximately 289 mm of travel for 1000 mean counts. This numerical example is illustrative.</p>
                <figure class="plot"><img src="./assets/images/odometry/radius_calibration.png" alt="Illustrative distance versus mean encoder counts and measured-minus-estimated residuals, comparing nominal- and fitted-radius predictions with separate held-back observations" /><figcaption>Figure 1. Synthetic calibration observations compare predictions made with nominal and fitted effective radii. Crosses represent held-back distances excluded from fitting; both panels retain the repeated observations.</figcaption></figure>
                <p>The left panel shows how changing the effective radius changes predicted travel. The right panel makes the discrepancy easier to see using a <strong>residual</strong>, measured distance minus estimated distance: \(e_d=d_{\mathrm{measured}}-\hat{d}\). A positive residual means the model underestimated travel. Residuals close to zero without a repeatable trend support the revised radius over the tested range. Their size must still be judged against the placement and reading uncertainty measured in this exercise.</p>
                <p>A fitted effective radius need not equal a calliper measurement of the unloaded wheel. Tyre compression and repeatable interaction with the test surface can change the operational distance travelled per revolution, while slip can make that relationship vary between trials. Because the encoder resolution is specified as fixed, keep <code>encoderCountsPerRevolution</code> unchanged and attribute the fitted correction to an effective radius under the stated test conditions. Change both <code>leftWheelRadiusMm</code> and <code>rightWheelRadiusMm</code> together for this first model; do not claim that the result isolates manufacturing tolerance from every other physical effect.</p>
                <p>The supplied <code>robot.startMoveDistance(...)</code> helper can produce nominal movements while your acquisition routine runs. Start the helper once, then log data and poll <code>robot.getMotionStatus()</code>; the requested distance and completion state describe the helper, not the physical result. Use a dedicated acquisition routine with no competing motor commands, keep a clear test area and accessible stop, and remember that <code>robot.setMotorPWM(0, 0)</code> cancels the helper.</p>
                <p>Use nearly straight trials so that signed forward displacement is a reasonable measure of path length. For a visibly curved run, straight-line endpoint distance is not wheel travel; retain it as evidence for Exercise 4 rather than forcing it into the simple fit. Applying the new radius and testing the Euler output are part of this calibration exercise.</p>
				  -->              
