                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Exercise 2 guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Self-Directed Route</button>
                    <button class="context-tab" data-context="ai" role="tab" aria-selected="false">AI-Assisted Route</button>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
                    <h3>Investigation brief</h3>
                    <ol>
                      <li><strong>Check and define the reference.</strong> Verify at least one printed dimension with a ruler and note any printer-scaling discrepancy. Record the supplied world axes, positive heading, chosen robot-centre marker, viewing method, and practical reading resolution.</li>
                      <li><strong>Measure placement error.</strong> Place and remove the stationary robot at least five times at the same nominal start pose. Keep each observed x, y, and heading rather than only their average. Calculate their spread and identify one systematic error that this repeatability test might not reveal.</li>
                      <li><strong>Plan the comparison.</strong> Select several safely achievable travel distances with repeats, including forward and reverse where practical. Reserve at least one distance condition for validation before fitting. Record starting and final physical pose, raw encoder counts, nominal model output, surface, battery condition, and payload.</li>
                      <li><strong>Run one helper safely.</strong> Start <code>robot.startMoveDistance(...)</code> once, then continue logging and poll <code>robot.getMotionStatus()</code> for completion, timeout, or cancellation. Do not restart it in each loop iteration. Ensure that no controller or other routine issues competing PWM commands.</li>
                      <li><strong>Estimate the effective radius.</strong> Calculate signed mean count changes and plot independently measured distance against them. Using only the calibration conditions, fit \(\hat{d}=mq\), calculate \(r_{\mathrm{eff}}=mN/(2\pi)\), and report \(g_r=r_{\mathrm{eff}}/r_{\mathrm{nom}}\). A line with an intercept can help diagnose a start/reference offset, but the supplied radius model assumes zero distance for zero count change; explain any substantial intercept rather than hiding it in a radius adjustment.</li>
                      <li><strong>Quantify uncertainty.</strong> Keep \(N\) fixed and compare effective-radius estimates across repeated trials, or refit while leaving out one whole trial at a time. Compare calibration residuals with the placement/read spread and explain how the selected distance range affects the result.</li>
                      <li><strong>Apply the calibration.</strong> Set both radius fields on <code>controller.odometry</code> to the fitted \(r_{\mathrm{eff}}\). Reset the local pose and obtain a successful count baseline while stationary before the next run. Keep the separation and encoder resolution unchanged at this stage, and save all parameter values with the result.</li>
                      <li><strong>Compare like with like.</strong> Plot measured versus estimated distance and measured-minus-estimated residuals before and after calibration. To compare both parameter sets on exactly the same motion, replay the same complete encoder log through the Euler equations in Python, checking one interval against the class. A replay from sparse telemetry may differ from the faster live integration during curves; record the sampling used.</li>
                      <li><strong>Validate and interpret.</strong> Freeze the radius and test the reserved distance. Apply the model to that trial's measured counts and compare with its independent physical outcome. Report the residual and uncertainty without refitting to that observation. If error depends on direction, distance, or slip, state where the shared effective-radius model remains useful and what needs further investigation.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <strong>Expected outcome</strong>
                    <p>A checked external reference and placement-error estimate, followed by a shared effective radius with units, uncertainty, before/after residuals, and a held-back distance test using the supplied model.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel" data-panel="ai" hidden>
                    <strong>AI-assisted option</strong>
                    <p>Provide AI with the repeated-placement observations, raw trial groups, nominal radius, fixed encoder resolution, and reserved conditions. Ask for a transparent radius estimate and residual plot; check that it does not tune \(N\), confuse placement variation with movement variation, or include validation rows in the fit.</p>
                    <div class="prompt-snippet">“Use these repeated placements to summarise the measurement resolution, then fit measured signed distance against mean encoder change using only the calibration trials. Keep N fixed, calculate the effective shared radius and its dimensionless correction from the nominal radius, retain all repeats, and evaluate the frozen radius on the reserved distance.”</div>
                    <span class="ai-reminder">Your raw observations, checked calculations, and independent physical reference determine what the result supports.</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="investigation exercise-evidence">
                  <div class="measure-row"><span><b>Measure</b>placement error</span><span><b>Fit</b>shared wheel radius</span><span><b>Validate</b>unused distance</span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-03" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">03</div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div><span class="concept-tag">Rotation · effective geometry</span><h2>Wheel separation and heading calibration</h2></div>
                <span class="time-badge">40–55 min</span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p><strong>Goal:</strong> estimate effective wheel separation from observed turns, update the supplied model, and test heading estimates in both directions.</p>
                <p>When the wheels travel equal distances in the same direction, the differential-drive model predicts no heading change. When one wheel travels farther, the robot turns. The same difference in wheel travel produces a smaller turn on a robot whose wheels are farther apart. The distance between the wheel contacts therefore controls the scale of the model's heading estimate.</p>
                <p>Using the calibrated wheel radii from Exercise 2, calculate signed wheel travel and compare its difference with the externally measured angle:</p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Heading change equals differential wheel travel divided by separation; separation equals differential travel divided by observed heading">
                \[\Delta\hat{\theta}=\frac{s_R-s_L}{B},\qquad B=\frac{s_R-s_L}{\Delta\theta_{\mathrm{measured}}}\]
                </div>
                <p>Angles must be in radians in this equation. For example, a differential travel of 90 mm over an observed turn of 1 radian gives an effective separation of 90 mm. Positive and negative turns should give a positive separation when wheel and heading signs agree. Near-zero turns make the division very sensitive to reference error, so several clearly measurable turn sizes are more informative than one tiny rotation.</p>
                <figure class="plot"><img src="./assets/images/odometry/separation_calibration.png" alt="Illustrative measured heading against signed differential wheel travel, with nominal and fitted predictions and their residuals for clockwise and anticlockwise turns" /><figcaption>Figure 2. Synthetic turn observations show the effect of revising separation. The fit excludes the turn conditions marked with crosses. Residuals are measured heading minus estimated heading.</figcaption></figure>
                <p>A separation that is too large makes the predicted magnitude of each turn too small. In Figure 2 the nominal residual therefore changes sign when the turn direction reverses. Compare this repeatable pattern with the remaining spread after fitting. A direction-dependent pattern can justify another investigation, but it does not alone identify wheel geometry: slip, radius error, the heading reference, and the stopping motion can all contribute.</p>
                <p>In <code>OdometryModel.h</code>, <code>wheelSeparationMm</code> controls the calculation of <code>deltaTheta</code>. It represents one <strong>effective separation</strong>, conditional on your wheel scales and tested surface. A changed separation also changes future x and y updates because they use the accumulated heading. Small heading errors can therefore become substantial lateral errors during later forward travel.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">
                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Exercise 3 guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Self-Directed Route</button>
                    <button class="context-tab" data-context="ai" role="tab" aria-selected="false">AI-Assisted Route</button>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
                    <h3>Investigation brief</h3>
                    <ol>
                      <li><strong>Collect signed turn evidence.</strong> Reuse the acquisition routine and start <code>robot.startRotateAngle(...)</code> once for each trial. Request at least three turn magnitudes in each direction within a safe range. Repeat trials, use the printed sheet to record start and final headings, and reserve a turn condition for validation. Record timeout or cancellation instead of treating it as successful completion.</li>
                      <li><strong>Check conventions.</strong> Calculate both signed wheel distances from counts and the calibrated radii. Verify the sign of \(s_R-s_L\) against the physical turn. Convert degrees to radians with \(\theta_{\mathrm{rad}}=\theta_{\mathrm{deg}}\pi/180\). For turns crossing a display boundary, unwrap the observations consistently rather than subtracting wrapped headings blindly.</li>
                      <li><strong>Estimate separation.</strong> Calculate B for each calibration run and compare turn size and direction. Alternatively, fit observed heading against differential travel through the origin; its slope is \(1/B\). Keep individual results visible and explain any non-zero intercept or direction-dependent discrepancy.</li>
                      <li><strong>Use the model.</strong> Enter the selected value into <code>controller.odometry.wheelSeparationMm</code>, reset while stationary, and acquire a new baseline. Hand-check one turning update, including the old heading used for x and y. Compare nominal and calibrated estimates on the same logs where possible.</li>
                      <li><strong>Evaluate sensitivity.</strong> Plot heading residuals against signed measured angle or differential travel. Recalculate using plausible wheel-radius and reference-angle variations from Exercise 2. Report the range of separation values the evidence supports.</li>
                      <li><strong>Test the reserved turn.</strong> Apply the fixed model to its encoder record and compare with the external heading. Preserve the original result if you later revise the model. Use any repeated direction-dependent error to plan Exercise 4.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <strong>Expected outcome</strong>
                    <p>An effective separation tied to the calibrated wheel scales and physical conditions, with uncertainty, signed residuals, and a held-back turn comparison.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel" data-panel="ai" hidden>
                    <strong>AI-assisted option</strong>
                    <p>Ask AI to check signs and radians, compare per-trial estimates with a fitted slope, and plot both directions. Verify one B calculation independently.</p>
                    <div class="prompt-snippet">“Using these calibrated wheel radii and signed encoder and external-heading trials, estimate one effective separation. Exclude the reserved turn, show measured-minus-estimated residuals, and test sensitivity to radius and heading uncertainty.”</div>
                    <span class="ai-reminder">Your raw observations, checked calculations, and independent physical reference determine what the result supports.</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="investigation exercise-evidence">
                  <div class="measure-row"><span><b>Vary</b>turn size + direction</span><span><b>Fit</b>effective separation</span><span><b>Validate</b>unused turn</span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-04" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">04</div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div><span class="concept-tag">Model comparison · physical causes</span><h2>Left/right wheel differences</h2></div>
                <span class="time-badge">40–55 min</span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p><strong>Goal:</strong> decide whether the combined distance and heading evidence supports different effective wheel radii, and whether that additional parameter improves the supplied model on unused trials.</p>
                <p>A robot that bends away from a straight line invites an obvious explanation: perhaps one wheel travels farther per revolution. But equal requested PWM does not guarantee equal wheel rotation, as the motor investigation shows. The encoder record helps separate those cases. If one wheel accumulates more counts, the shared-radius model already predicts a turn. If the counts are equal but a repeatable physical turn remains, different distance-per-count scales become one candidate explanation.</p>
                <figure class="plot"><img src="./assets/images/odometry/asymmetry_candidates.png" alt="Two illustrative trajectory panels show the same curved path arising from equal counts with unequal radii or unequal counts with equal radii, each compared with a straight reference" /><figcaption>Figure 3. Two ideal rolling examples produce the same curved trajectory through different combinations of wheel rotation and radius. The path alone cannot distinguish them; the encoder record provides additional evidence.</figcaption></figure>
                <p>Figure 3 is deliberately a comparison of possible causes, not a diagnosis of your robot. Slip can also change the relation between wheel rotation and ground travel. A start-heading error rotates the apparent trajectory, while an incorrectly located chassis marker can shift the apparent endpoint. Repeated observations in both directions, with the initial pose recorded, help determine which explanation deserves further testing.</p>
                <p>The more flexible model uses one distance scale for each wheel:</p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Separate wheel distances use separate radii and the same stated encoder counts per revolution">
                \[s_L=\frac{2\pi r_L}{N}\Delta n_L,\qquad
                s_R=\frac{2\pi r_R}{N}\Delta n_R\]
                </div>
                <p>It has an extra freedom relative to the shared-radius model: the ratio \(r_R/r_L\). To keep the first comparison understandable, you can preserve the calibrated mean radius \(\bar r\) and vary a small fractional difference \(\epsilon\): \(r_L=\bar r(1-\epsilon)\), \(r_R=\bar r(1+\epsilon)\). At \(\epsilon=0\), this is your existing shared-radius model. Test candidates on the same encoder logs through the same Euler update.</p>
                <p>One endpoint or one shallow curve cannot establish all of the geometry. Use both distance and turn records from Exercises 2 and 3, compare x, y, and heading against their respective reference uncertainties, and check whether a claimed improvement exceeds repeat-to-repeat variation. Because separation was estimated using the previous radii, revisit it if the scales change. A shared model that predicts adequately is a valid outcome, just as a straight line may remain adequate in the motor and reflectance model comparisons.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">
                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Exercise 4 guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Self-Directed Route</button>
                    <button class="context-tab" data-context="ai" role="tab" aria-selected="false">AI-Assisted Route</button>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
                    <h3>Investigation brief</h3>
                    <ol>
                      <li><strong>Audit existing evidence.</strong> Plot the shared-model trajectories and measured endpoints from earlier trials. Where only final physical poses were measured, show endpoint markers; do not draw an invented measured trajectory. Inspect left/right count differences, initial headings, and measured-minus-estimated x, y, and heading residuals.</li>
                      <li><strong>Resolve a specific ambiguity.</strong> Add only the trials needed to test a repeatable discrepancy: for example, forward and reverse travel or the same movement from a different marked initial orientation. Keep surface and load controlled, and preserve both directions' data.</li>
                      <li><strong>Compare candidates.</strong> Retain the shared-radius model as the baseline. Test a stated range of separate-radius candidates in Python using the same calibration logs and initial poses. Start with a fixed mean radius and separation. Report distance errors in millimetres and heading errors in radians or degrees; if combining them into one score, scale by stated tolerances rather than adding unlike units directly.</li>
                      <li><strong>Check parameter dependence.</strong> For a supported radius change, re-estimate separation using calibration turns and recheck translation. State whether several parameter combinations fit about equally well. If your data cannot distinguish them, retain a simpler model or report the unresolved range.</li>
                      <li><strong>Apply and validate.</strong> Put the selected radii and separation into <code>controller.odometry</code>, reset and baseline it before the trial, and test a movement condition excluded from all tuning. Compare both models on that same new encoder record against the same external observation.</li>
                      <li><strong>Explain the result.</strong> Report whether separate radii improve prediction enough to justify the added parameter. Describe the surface, direction, travel range, uncertainty, and remaining error. Preserve unsuccessful candidates and do not infer physical tyre dimensions solely from a fitted effective radius.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <strong>Expected outcome</strong>
                    <p>A justified shared- or separate-radius model, traceable parameter choices, and validation showing whether extra complexity produces a meaningful improvement.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel" data-panel="ai" hidden>
                    <strong>AI-assisted option</strong>
                    <p>Ask AI to compare the candidates using identical logs and held-back trials. Require it to identify parameter ambiguity and distinguish count imbalance from scale imbalance.</p>
                    <div class="prompt-snippet">“Compare shared and separate wheel radii on these timestamped encoder logs and measured endpoints. Keep calibration and validation trials separate, show position and heading residuals with units, and identify geometry combinations my measurements cannot distinguish.”</div>
                    <span class="ai-reminder">Your raw observations, checked calculations, and independent physical reference determine what the result supports.</span>
                  </div>
