              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-05" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">05</div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div><span class="concept-tag">Inertial measurement · accumulated error</span><h2>Gyroscope bias and heading drift</h2></div>
                <span class="time-badge">45–60 min</span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p><strong>Goal:</strong> measure the gyroscope's stationary bias, examine how it affects integrated heading, and compare gyro and encoder estimates against the same physical reference.</p>
                <p>The encoders infer turning from the wheels. A gyroscope responds to rotation of the robot body and provides another way to estimate heading, including situations where wheel slip makes encoder odometry doubtful. It measures <strong>angular velocity</strong>, however, so its readings must be accumulated over time to obtain an angle.</p>
                <p>A stationary gyro may report values scattered around a small non-zero mean. The mean is a candidate <strong>zero-rate bias</strong>; the scatter describes variation about that mean. If a constant bias of 0.2 degrees/s remained in an estimate for 20 seconds, it would contribute 4 degrees of heading error. Small rate errors can therefore matter even when a raw signal looks almost flat.</p>
                <figure class="plot"><img src="./assets/images/odometry/gyro_drift.png" alt="Synthetic stationary gyro rate and integrated heading show that bias correction reduces accumulated drift on a segment excluded from bias estimation" /><figcaption>Figure 4. A synthetic stationary record is divided into bias-calibration and evaluation segments. The second panel integrates only the evaluation segment; it does not reuse that segment to force its final error to zero.</figcaption></figure>
                <p>The left panel separates a typical rate from sample variation. The right panel shows why subtracting an independently estimated bias can improve heading, while leaving residual drift. Estimating bias from the entire evaluated stationary record would make its total corrected angle approximately zero by construction. That would provide weak evidence that the correction generalises.</p>
                <p>In <code>RobotIMU.h</code>, <code>imu.isSampleReady()</code> checks data readiness, <code>imu.read()</code> acquires a sample, and <code>imu.getSample()</code> exposes the raw axes, sequence number, and <code>timestamp_us</code>. <code>RobotIMU_c::gyroscopeToDegreesPerSecond(...)</code> converts the configured raw gyro values. These direct IMU measurements do not pass through the opaque middleware. The wrapper does not estimate bias or integrate heading for you.</p>
                <p>For a simple left-endpoint integration, use the previous corrected rate over the measured sample interval:</p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Gyro heading equals previous heading plus previous measured rate minus estimated bias times elapsed seconds">
                \[\hat{\theta}_{k+1}=\hat{\theta}_k+(\omega_k-\hat b)\Delta t_k,\qquad
                \Delta t_k=(t_{k+1}-t_k)\times10^{-6}\]
                </div>
                <p>Here the timestamps are in microseconds, \(\omega\) and \(\hat b\) are in degrees/s, and this calculated heading is in degrees. Convert the encoder model's radians before comparing them. The IMU timestamp is taken after the reads on the StampC3, not at an exactly shared sampling instant with the encoders. Keep each source's acquisition time and align the records explicitly.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">
                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Exercise 5 guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Self-Directed Route</button>
                    <button class="context-tab" data-context="ai" role="tab" aria-selected="false">AI-Assisted Route</button>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
                    <h3>Investigation brief</h3>
                    <ol>
                      <li><strong>Prepare one acquisition routine.</strong> Follow <code>imu.initialise()</code> in the top-level sketch and the existing IMU example. Check <code>imu.isAvailable()</code>. Use <code>if (imu.isSampleReady() &amp;&amp; imu.read())</code> before recording a sample. Preserve all three raw gyro channels, sequence numbers, and timestamps, together with trial and warm-up information.</li>
                      <li><strong>Verify the yaw axis.</strong> With motor commands disabled, rotate the chassis gently by hand about its vertical axis. Identify the responding channel and its sign experimentally; rotating only a raised wheel is not a body-yaw test. Match positive gyro yaw to the physical heading convention from Exercise 2.</li>
                      <li><strong>Estimate bias and test it separately.</strong> Record at least 20 seconds stationary and reserve a later segment or separate trial. Estimate the rate mean and spread from the calibration portion. Integrate the evaluation portion with and without that fixed bias. Compare drift over time and repeat after a different warm-up duration.</li>
                      <li><strong>Check one integration.</strong> Convert a raw value and a timestamp difference by hand, then verify one angle increment. Handle timer rollover or restart explicitly; do not integrate across a restart or an unexplained gap. Retain raw data even if you later investigate smoothing.</li>
                      <li><strong>Compare turns.</strong> Extend the existing turn acquisition to include gyro readings and successful encoder updates, preserving their separate times. Repeat selected clockwise and anticlockwise turns, including an unused condition. Freeze the wheel geometry and gyro bias before evaluation. At the end of each trial compare both heading estimates with the external measurement.</li>
                      <li><strong>Explain disagreement.</strong> Plot time traces and measured-minus-estimated final heading residuals. Ask whether discrepancies grow with elapsed time, turn size, direction, or surface condition. Where there is no continuous external record, do not claim that one onboard time trace proves the other wrong. Propose one changed trial that would distinguish two plausible explanations.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <strong>Expected outcome</strong>
                    <p>A measured gyro bias and its validity conditions, a separate stationary drift test, and an uncertainty-aware comparison of gyro, encoder, and external heading.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel" data-panel="ai" hidden>
                    <strong>AI-assisted option</strong>
                    <p>Ask AI to inspect units, bias-calibration boundaries, timing alignment, and residual definitions. Check one numerical update and retain competing explanations for disagreement.</p>
                    <div class="prompt-snippet">“Compare these gyro and encoder heading estimates with the external reference. Estimate gyro bias only from the designated stationary calibration record. Preserve separate timestamps, convert units explicitly, and evaluate held-back stationary and turning records without refitting.”</div>
                    <span class="ai-reminder">Your raw observations, checked calculations, and independent physical reference determine what the result supports.</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="investigation exercise-evidence">
                  <div class="measure-row"><span><b>Measure</b>rate bias + spread</span><span><b>Integrate</b>actual sample intervals</span><span><b>Compare</b>two estimates + reference</span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
          <article id="exercise-06" class="exercise-card">
            <div class="exercise-index">06</div>
            <div class="exercise-content">
              <div class="exercise-heading">
                <div><span class="concept-tag">Inertial measurement · double integration</span><h2>Going Further: acceleration and displacement</h2></div>
                <span class="time-badge">45–60 min</span>
              </div>
              <div class="teaching-brief">
                <p><strong>Goal:</strong> investigate what happens when measured acceleration is integrated into velocity and displacement, and identify the conditions under which the estimate is useful or unreliable.</p>
                <p>Exercise 5 accumulated angular velocity once to estimate heading. For translation, we might hope to accumulate acceleration into velocity and then accumulate velocity into position. This involves <strong>two integrations</strong>. A small persistent acceleration error changes the estimated velocity at every step; the growing velocity error then changes position increasingly quickly.</p>
                <p>For a constant uncorrected acceleration error b, starting with zero velocity and position error, the continuous relationships below describe estimated minus actual velocity and position. A positive bias therefore leads to an overestimate:</p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Constant acceleration error produces velocity error b times time and position error one half b times time squared">
                \[e_v(t)=bt,\qquad e_x(t)=\tfrac12bt^2\]
                </div>
                <p>For example, an error of only 0.01 m/s² contributes 0.5 m of displacement error after 10 seconds. These are error-growth predictions under a stated constant-error assumption, not expected specifications for your sensor.</p>
                <figure class="plot"><img src="./assets/images/odometry/acceleration_integration.png" alt="Illustrative acceleration errors close to zero produce growing velocity and displacement errors after two integrations; a constant bias plus random variation drifts much more than zero-mean variation alone" /><figcaption>Figure 5. Synthetic stationary signals illustrate how small acceleration errors accumulate. The dotted displacement curve shows the constant-bias prediction; the other curves are calculated from the time samples.</figcaption></figure>
                <p>The figure explains why a smooth position curve is not evidence of accurate displacement. Smoothing may reduce rapid variation while leaving a persistent bias largely unchanged. The sensor also measures <strong>specific force</strong>: its output depends on orientation relative to gravity as well as motion. A stationary sensor can report roughly one g along a vertical axis. A slight tilt projects part of that stationary signal onto an axis intended to measure horizontal motion.</p>
                <p><code>RobotIMU_c::accelerationToG(...)</code> converts the raw acceleration values to units of g. Multiply by approximately 9.81 to obtain m/s². Subtracting a stationary mean can provide a baseline for a fixed orientation, but does not remove changing gravity projection during a moving or tilting trial. Begin with stationary records and short, level, approximately straight movements rather than attempting unrestricted inertial navigation.</p>
                <p>A transparent numerical starting point uses the previous acceleration and velocity over each recorded interval:</p>
                <div class="equation" role="img" aria-label="Velocity is updated from previous acceleration, and displacement from previous velocity">
                \[\hat v_{k+1}=\hat v_k+a_k\Delta t_k,\qquad
                \hat d_{k+1}=\hat d_k+\hat v_k\Delta t_k\]
                </div>
                <p>Here \(a_k\) is your converted, baseline-adjusted acceleration in the chosen direction. This is a simple numerical approximation, so state the update order, initial velocity, and initial position. The supplied wheel odometry class does not perform this calculation; use a small Python analysis of the IMU records. Retain wheel odometry as another estimate and the supplied printed sheet as the independent comparison.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="exercise-grid">
                <div class="context-box" data-context-box>
                  <div class="context-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Exercise 6 guidance">
                    <button class="context-tab active" data-context="nonai" role="tab" aria-selected="true">Self-Directed Route</button>
                    <button class="context-tab" data-context="ai" role="tab" aria-selected="false">AI-Assisted Route</button>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel active" data-panel="nonai">
                    <h3>Investigation brief</h3>
                    <ol>
                      <li><strong>Reuse the IMU logger.</strong> Include all three raw acceleration channels, timestamps, and sequence numbers. Tilt the stationary chassis gently to establish the physical axes. Return it to the documented test orientation and record at least 10 seconds without motion.</li>
                      <li><strong>Separate baseline and variation.</strong> Report the mean and spread for each axis and inspect time traces. Choose an approximately horizontal axis aligned with the proposed straight movement. Estimate its baseline from a designated calibration record and retain a separate stationary record for evaluation.</li>
                      <li><strong>Integrate stationary evidence first.</strong> Convert to m/s² and seconds, set initial velocity and displacement explicitly to zero, and integrate the evaluation record twice. Hand-check the first updates. Plot acceleration, velocity, and displacement, comparing the implied motion with the known stationary condition.</li>
                      <li><strong>Test a short movement.</strong> Begin and finish stationary, record raw IMU and encoder data, and measure travel independently. Apply the fixed baseline and calculation without adjusting them to match the final displacement. Convert encoder millimetres and inertial metres to common units before comparing.</li>
                      <li><strong>Investigate one limitation.</strong> Choose a changed duration, orientation, or one documented smoothing method according to the observed error. Keep the original result. Test the change on a separate record; do not force final velocity to zero unless you explicitly introduce and justify that additional constraint.</li>
                      <li><strong>State what the evidence supports.</strong> Distinguish bias, changing orientation, vibration, initial-condition error, timing, and numerical integration as possible contributors. An unresolved or poor displacement estimate is a valid result when the records and calculations show its limits. Explain what would be needed before using it in a Digital Twin or controller.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <strong>Expected outcome</strong>
                    <p>Reproducible acceleration, velocity, and displacement plots, with an evidence-based account of error growth and the limits of the tested method.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div class="context-panel" data-panel="ai" hidden>
                    <strong>AI-assisted option</strong>
                    <p>Ask AI for an inspectable integration calculation with explicit units and update order. Require it to preserve raw records and distinguish fixed mean subtraction from orientation compensation.</p>
                    <div class="prompt-snippet">“Integrate these acceleration records using the separately measured stationary baseline and actual timestamp differences. Show initial conditions and units. Compare stationary and moving results with external measurements, and do not impose zero final velocity or displacement to improve the plot.”</div>
                    <span class="ai-reminder">Your raw observations, checked calculations, and independent physical reference determine what the result supports.</span>
                  </div>
                </div>
                <div class="investigation exercise-evidence">
                  <div class="measure-row"><span><b>Inspect</b>stationary acceleration</span><span><b>Calculate</b>velocity + displacement</span><span><b>Explain</b>error growth + limits</span></div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>
        <section id="going-further" class="selection-section">
          <div class="section-heading"><span class="section-number">+</span><div><span class="section-kicker">Optional investigations</span><h2>Going Further with odometry</h2></div>
</div>
          <p class="section-lead">Exercise 6 provides an optional acceleration investigation. The further activities below can extend a calibrated model or support Phase 2. Select an activity because it addresses a limitation found in your evidence.</p>
          <div class="subsystem-grid">
            <article class="subsystem-card"><span class="section-number">01</span><h3>Curves and asymmetric geometry</h3>
            <p>Use left- and right-turning arcs to test the calibrated wheel radii and effective separation beyond straight and in-place motions. Relate endpoint errors to slip, the physical reference point, and the integration step. Do not infer separate centre-to-wheel distances from turning angle alone.</p>
</article>
            <article class="subsystem-card"><span class="section-number">02</span><h3>Exact differential-drive integration</h3>
            <p>Implement exact constant-curvature integration and compare it with Euler using the same encoder log. Decide whether the difference is meaningful relative to reference uncertainty.</p>
</article>
            <article class="subsystem-card"><span class="section-number">03</span><h3>Bidirectional square benchmark</h3>
            <p>Run repeatable clockwise and counter-clockwise squares to expose accumulated systematic error. Use this as a diagnostic, not as the first place to fit every parameter.</p>
</article>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="validation" id="validation">
          <div><span class="section-kicker">Before Phase 2</span><h2>Carry a transparent model forward</h2>
          <p>Carry forward raw encoder records, the supplied physical reference and its uncertainty, calibrated geometry, initial-pose conventions, and before/after residuals from unused distance and turn conditions. Include gyro bias and drift evidence, and acceleration findings if you undertook Exercise 6. Record limitations as well as improvements so the team can decide how to use each estimate in Phase 2.</p>
</div>
          <div class="validation-list"><span>□ Check the supplied reference</span><span>□ Calibrate encoder geometry</span><span>□ Compare gyro heading and drift</span><span>□ Validate on unused conditions</span></div>
